from . import T
T('4-analysis','bounds','Big O;Theta;Omega;Asymptotic worst case time','Asymptotic bounds and worst-case functions',r'''
A running-time function counts operations as input size grows. Worst-case T(n) maximizes that count over legal inputs of size n; best and average case are different functions. Big O bounds growth from above, Ω from below and Θ from both, after some threshold using positive constants. A tight Θ(n²) function is also O(n³), so “an O bound” is not necessarily its most informative one.
Separate the input distribution from the asymptotic notation. Ω does not inherently mean best case, and O does not inherently mean worst case: either can bound a chosen best, worst or average function. Ignore constant factors and lower-order terms only after deriving or bounding work.
Nested loop bounds may depend on each other; sum iteration counts rather than automatically multiplying n by n. Logarithmic work typically arises from repeatedly shrinking a range by a constant factor. Express complexity in the stated input-size measure: a numeric parameter can be exponentially large relative to its binary encoding length, which makes pseudopolynomial distinctions relevant.
''','Find tight growth of T(n)=3n²+5n+7.',r'Lower bound::T(n)\ge3n^2::All terms are nonnegative for n≥1.~Upper bound::T(n)\le15n^2\quad(n\ge1)::Use n≤n² and 1≤n².~Conclude::T(n)=\Theta(n^2)::Both constant-factor bounds hold.', 'Θ(n²); O(n³) is true but loose.','The conclusion concerns the supplied T, regardless of whether it represents worst or average case.')
T('4-analysis','space','Space complexity','Auxiliary space, live allocations and recursion',r'''
Space complexity describes memory needed at peak execution, not the sum of all memory ever allocated over time. Auxiliary space commonly excludes input storage but includes work structures and recursion frames; output inclusion depends on the convention. Report what is counted. An algorithm can use linear time while storing only constant extra state.
For recursion, count the longest active call chain and storage retained in each frame. Sequential recursive branches reuse stack capacity after returning; adding the full sizes of separate finished subtrees overestimates peak stack. Large local buffers can dominate even at small depth. Iterative simulation may replace call frames with an explicit stack without changing the needed information.
In-place algorithms use only limited extra storage under their defined convention; recursive quicksort can have in-place partitioning and still use stack space. Memoization adds stored subproblem results and can exchange memory for time. Distinguish O(1) references to an existing input from creating an O(n) copy of its contents; a slice or allocation’s actual semantics matter.
''','A binary search is implemented iteratively with low, high and mid indices.',r'Existing input::::The array is supplied and need not be copied.~Working state::::Only a constant number of numeric indices and comparison variables are live.~Bound::S_{aux}=O(1)::The number of work variables is independent of n.', 'Constant auxiliary space.','A recursive version commonly needs O(log n) call frames unless the implementation removes them.')
T('4-search-sort','search','Searching;Binary search','Linear search and binary-search invariants',r'''
Linear search examines candidates one by one and works without sorted order, taking O(n) worst-case comparisons. Binary search uses sorted order to discard approximately half the candidates after a midpoint comparison, giving O(log n). The operation must support efficient midpoint access; repeatedly traversing a linked list changes the cost model.
For an inclusive interval [low,high], a smaller midpoint value rules out indices through mid, so set low=mid+1; a larger value sets high=mid−1. Stop when low>high. A half-open convention uses different boundaries and updates. State the invariant that any remaining target lies in the candidate interval and ensure each unsuccessful iteration shrinks it.
Duplicate targets require a modified boundary search for first/last occurrence; ordinary equality return may find any occurrence. Compute mid as low+(high−low)/2 to avoid overflowing a bounded addition. Binary search is also useful on a monotone feasibility predicate, but monotonicity must be proved. GATE trace errors commonly arise from combining two interval conventions or failing to remove the tested midpoint.
''','Find 8 in sorted [2,4,6,8,10] with inclusive bounds.',r'First midpoint::low=0,\ high=4,\ mid=2::Value 6 is smaller than 8.~Shrink rightward::low=3,\ high=4,\ mid=3::Indices 0 through 2 cannot contain the target.~Match::::Value at index 3 is 8; return 3.', 'Zero-based index 3.','The successful interval contains the target, and the first update removed all proven-too-small values.')
T('4-search-sort','merge','Sorting;Merge sort','Merge sort and stable merging',r'''
Sorting arranges items by a key; a stable sort preserves the original relative order of equal keys. Merge sort splits a sequence, recursively sorts the halves, then merges by comparing the front unconsumed elements. The merge reads/moves each element at most a constant number of times, costing Θ(n) for combined length n.
Balanced recursion gives T(n)=2T(n/2)+Θ(n), so total work is Θ(n log n). A conventional array implementation uses Θ(n) auxiliary merge storage, while recursion depth is logarithmic. Stability requires choosing the left-side item first on equal keys when merging already stable halves. A different tie rule can change equal-key order.
The comparison-sorting lower bound is Ω(n log n) in the general worst-case decision-tree model; it does not rule out linear integer sorts with stronger key assumptions. GATE problems may ask comparisons for a particular merge rather than asymptotic sort time: once one side is exhausted, appending the remainder needs no further cross-side comparison.
''','Merge [1,4,7] and [2,3,8].',r'Front comparisons::::Take 1, then 2, then 3, leaving [4,7] and [8].~Finish comparisons::::Take 4, then 7, exhausting the left side.~Append remainder::::Append 8 without another cross-list comparison.', 'Sorted result [1,2,3,4,7,8], using five cross-list comparisons.','Each source preserves its relative order, and all six elements appear exactly once.')
T('4-search-sort','quick','Quicksort','Quicksort partitions and bad pivot sequences',r'''
Quicksort selects a pivot and partitions items so each belongs to an appropriate side of it, then recursively sorts those sides. Partition costs Θ(n), but exact array trace and pivot position depend on the stated scheme. Lomuto and Hoare conventions differ; one cannot assume the pivot lands in a final position under every partition algorithm.
Balanced splits give Θ(n log n), while repeatedly splitting into zero and n−1 gives Θ(n²). Random pivot selection gives expected Θ(n log n) under the usual model but does not remove the worst-case possibility. Sorted data is especially bad only for certain deterministic pivot choices, not for every quicksort implementation.
A conventional array partition uses constant local space but recursive stack can be O(n) under bad splits. Recursing on the smaller side and looping over the larger can limit stack depth. Standard quicksort is not stable. With duplicates, a three-way partition can isolate equal keys and avoid repeatedly reconsidering them, but complexity claims must follow the actual algorithm described.
''','Always choose the first element as pivot on [1,2,3,4].',r'First split::::Pivot 1 leaves sizes 0 and 3.~Subsequent splits::::Pivots 2 and 3 again leave empty left sides and sizes 2 and 1.~Work pattern::T(n)=T(n-1)+\Theta(n)=\Theta(n^2)::Linear partitions accumulate across linear recursion depth.', 'This pivot sequence gives quadratic time.','The input being sorted is harmful here because of the first-pivot policy; a middle or randomized pivot can behave differently.')
T('4-search-sort','heapsort','Heapsort','Heapsort’s ordered suffix and heap prefix',r'''
Heapsort first builds a max-heap, then repeatedly exchanges its largest root with the last active element, reduces the heap size and repairs heap order downward. After each iteration, the suffix contains finalized largest values in sorted order and is excluded from further heap operations. The active prefix remains a heap, not a fully sorted list.
Bottom-up construction is Θ(n). Each of n removals can cost O(log n), giving Θ(n log n) worst-case sort time. An iterative array implementation uses constant auxiliary storage aside from the input and is conventionally unstable because swaps can reverse equal-key order. It differs from a priority queue that keeps inserting while extracting.
During a trace, heap size is separate from array length; a sift must not reintroduce elements already placed in the sorted suffix. Choose the larger child in a max-heap repair, not simply the left child. Comparison counts depend on node structure and implementation, while the asymptotic bound remains logarithmic per removal.
''','One heapsort iteration on max-heap [9,5,7,1,3].',r'Exchange root and end::::Array becomes [3,5,7,1,9]; 9 is finalized.~Shrink active heap::::Only indices 0 through 3 remain in the heap.~Sift root::::Choose larger child 7 and swap, giving [7,5,3,1,9].', 'Active heap [7,5,3,1], sorted suffix [9].','The suffix value 9 must not participate in the next sift or heap comparison.')
T('4-hashing','chaining','Hashing;Chaining;Load factor','Hashing, chaining and expected cost',r'''
A hash function maps keys to buckets; distinct keys may collide and therefore need a resolution mechanism. Chaining stores a collection of keys in each bucket. With n keys and m buckets, load factor α=n/m is the average chain length. Under suitable distribution assumptions, expected lookup cost is O(1+α), while all keys colliding can produce O(n) worst-case search.
Hash equality is not key equality: after selecting a bucket, compare actual keys. Duplicate-key handling is a separate dictionary policy. Rehashing after resizing must recalculate bucket indices because the modulus/capacity changed; simply copying old buckets to the same positions may violate lookup rules.
The expected bound assumes reasonably spread hashes, not a guarantee for every input. Chaining can use load factor above one because buckets contain multiple elements. Space includes bucket headers and stored nodes. If the question states adversarial hashes, a specific chain order, or an unsuccessful search, trace that exact setting rather than citing average constant time without justification.
''','Using h(k)=k mod 5, insert 7,12,3 by chaining.',r'Hash keys::h(7)=2,\quad h(12)=2,\quad h(3)=3::Two keys collide in bucket 2.~Store chains::::Bucket 2 contains 7 and 12; bucket 3 contains 3.~Search 12::::Locate bucket 2, then compare stored keys until 12 is found.', 'The collision is resolved by a two-element chain.','A lookup for 17 also maps to bucket 2 but must compare keys and report absence, not return 12 merely because its hash matches.')
T('4-hashing','probing','Open addressing','Open addressing, probe sequences and deletion',r'''
Open addressing stores keys directly in table slots and follows a deterministic probe sequence on collisions. Linear probing visits consecutive slots modulo m; quadratic probing uses quadratic offsets; double hashing uses a second hash as a step. Coverage depends on table size and step conditions, so not every formula reaches every slot.
Search retraces insertion and can stop at a never-used empty slot. A deleted slot must normally be marked with a tombstone so searches continue through it to keys placed later in the collision chain. Insertion can reuse a tombstone while still following duplicate-detection policy. An entirely full table needs a bounded complete-cycle failure rule.
Linear probing creates clusters, making future nearby hashes take long probe runs as load rises. Unlike chaining, open addressing cannot store more keys than available slots and typically keeps load below one. Resizing reinserts keys under the new capacity. GATE traces should identify initial occupancy, probe formula, wraparound, deleted markers and whether a lookup succeeds before counting probes.
''','Table size 5: insert 4,9,14 using linear probing, then delete 9 and search 14.',r'Insert::::4 goes to slot 4; 9 wraps to 0; 14 continues to 1.~Delete::::Mark slot 0 as a tombstone, retaining probe-chain continuation.~Search::::Start at 4, pass occupied 4 and deleted 0, then find 14 at 1.', 'Search succeeds at slot 1.','Replacing the tombstone by never-used empty would wrongly terminate search before reaching 14.')
T('4-greedy-divide','greedy','Greedy;Exchange argument','Greedy choices require a global correctness proof',r'''
A greedy algorithm makes a locally selected feasible choice and commits without examining all alternatives. It is correct only when a structural property lets that choice belong to an optimal solution. An exchange argument starts from an optimum and replaces its choice with the greedy one without reducing quality, then applies the same reasoning to the residual problem.
For maximum activity count, earliest finish is safe because replacing an optimal first activity by an earlier-finishing one leaves at least as much room afterward. Highest value/weight ratio is safe for fractional knapsack because items can be divided, but can fail for 0/1 items. Similar-looking problem names do not inherit each other’s proofs.
Before using greedy, identify objective, feasibility and tie handling, then provide the exchange or cut argument. A small counterexample is enough to disprove an unsupported rule. Feasible output alone does not establish optimality, and examples where a rule works do not prove it for every input. In GATE MCQs, changing “fractional” to “indivisible” often changes the correct technique.
''','Capacity 50; 0/1 items (weight,value)=(10,60),(20,100),(30,120).',r'Ratio choice::::Ratios are 6,5,4, so greedy picks the first two for value 160.~Better feasible choice::::Weights 20+30 fit exactly; their value is 220.~Compare::220>160::One counterexample disproves ratio-greedy optimality for 0/1 knapsack.', 'Ratio greedy fails on this instance.','With divisible items, taking part of the third item becomes legal and changes the argument.')
T('4-greedy-divide','divide','Divide and conquer','Divide, solve and combine with a recurrence',r'''
Divide-and-conquer breaks a problem into smaller instances, solves them recursively and combines their answers. The subproblems should be meaningfully smaller and the recursion must terminate. Merge sort halves the data and combines sorted halves; binary search discards one side and recurses only into the other. Their recurrences therefore have different child counts.
Write T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n) when there are a equal-sized subproblems of size n/b and nonrecursive work f(n). A recursion tree sums total work at each level and includes leaves. The Master theorem applies to this specific form with required conditions; unequal or size-minus-one recurrences need different analysis.
Combining can dominate or match the leaf work, so counting splits alone is insufficient. A recursion with repeated overlapping subproblems may benefit from memoization and is not automatically efficient because the word “divide” applies. Space counts concurrent live work, not the sum of all disjoint subproblem sizes over the whole execution. Include copying costs if slices are physically allocated.
''','Analyze T(n)=4T(n/2)+n with constant base work.',r'Level work::4^j(n/2^j)=n2^j::Total combine work grows down the tree.~Depth::j_{max}=\log_2n::Subproblems reach constant size.~Leaf count::4^{\log_2n}=n^2::Leaf work and summed level work have quadratic order.', 'T(n)=Θ(n²).','Compare n with n^(log₂4)=n²; the recursive leaf growth dominates linear combine cost.')
T('4-dynamic','states','Dynamic programming;Optimal substructure;Memoization','DP states, dependencies and memoization',r'''
Dynamic programming stores solutions to overlapping subproblems. Optimal substructure means an optimum can be assembled using appropriate optimal subproblem answers; overlap means naive recursion would recompute them. Define the state in a sentence before writing a table entry: it must contain enough information about the past to determine valid future decisions.
Memoization evaluates a recursive recurrence on demand and caches each state. Tabulation computes states in an order where dependencies are already available. These can implement the same recurrence with different execution and stack behavior. Base cases encode the empty/smallest problem and unreachable states must use an appropriate sentinel rather than default zero when zero might be a valid optimum.
Complexity is reachable state count times transition work, plus relevant setup. Memory compression is correct only if updates preserve dependency versions. For 0/1 knapsack, decreasing capacities keep previous-item states; increasing capacities can reuse the current item. An optimal table value need not uniquely determine a solution; reconstruction may require stored choices or comparisons along dependencies.
''','Use memoization for F(n)=F(n−1)+F(n−2), F(0)=0,F(1)=1, at n=5.',r'Define state::::F(k) stores the kth Fibonacci value, independent of the path that requested it.~Reuse subproblems::::Each of F(0) through F(5) is computed at most once; repeated requests read cached values.~Compute::F(2)=1,\ F(3)=2,\ F(4)=3,\ F(5)=5::Dependencies determine each value.', 'F(5)=5 with linear distinct-state work.','Naive recursion repeats states, while memoization adds O(n) stored results and at most O(n) recursion depth.')
T('4-dynamic','knapsack','Knapsack','0/1 knapsack: a complete include/exclude recurrence',r'''
A 0/1 knapsack chooses each item at most once, maximizing value under capacity W. Define dp[i,w] as maximum value using only the first i items with capacity at most w. Excluding item i gives dp[i−1,w]; including it, if wᵢ≤w, gives vᵢ+dp[i−1,w−wᵢ]. Their maximum covers both possible final decisions.
Initialize dp[0,w]=0 for nonnegative values under an at-most-capacity formulation. An exactly-filled formulation requires unreachable-state handling and different initialization. Processing increasing i makes preceding-row dependencies available. A one-array optimization uses decreasing w so included terms still refer to the old item prefix.
Time is O(nW), which is pseudopolynomial in numeric capacity because W takes only logarithmic input bits to encode. Fractional knapsack uses a different feasible set, and ratio sorting does not solve this recurrence. Recover selected items by comparing adjacent prefix rows or recording decisions; ties can give multiple optimal subsets. Verify both total weight and single-use constraints after reconstruction.
''','Capacity 4; items (2,3) and (3,4).',r'First item::::For capacities 2,3,4 the best value is 3; capacities 0,1 remain zero.~Second decision::dp[2,4]=\max(3,4+dp[1,1])=4::Include or exclude item two.~Check feasibility::::The chosen item has weight 3. Both together weigh 5 and are not feasible.', 'Optimal value 4 using the second item.','An ascending one-array update with the first item could incorrectly allow value 6 at capacity 4 by reusing it.')
T('4-graph-algorithms','objectives','Graph traversals','Choose a graph algorithm by objective and assumptions',r'''
Reachability asks which vertices can be reached; shortest path minimizes one source-to-destination route cost; a minimum spanning tree connects every vertex with minimum total selected edge weight. These are different optimization objects even though algorithms can all keep parent pointers. Decide direction, weight sign, connectivity and representation before selecting a method.
BFS supplies unweighted distances; DFS exposes nesting and ordering properties; MST algorithms apply to undirected weighted graphs; Dijkstra needs nonnegative weights; Bellman–Ford allows negative edges and reports reachable negative cycles. A disconnected undirected graph has a minimum spanning forest rather than a single all-vertex tree.
The output must include a correctness certificate appropriate to its goal: a traversal parent route, distance relaxation justification, or a cut/exchange argument. A visually short drawn edge does not specify numerical weight. Include isolated vertices when counting V. The same input can produce an MST and a shortest-path tree of different total weights, so do not substitute one objective’s answer for another.
''','Triangle AB=2, BC=2, AC=3: compare MST with shortest paths from A.',r'MST::::Choose AB and BC for total 4; two cheaper edges connect all vertices.~Source distances::::From A, B costs 2; C’s direct edge costs 3 rather than 4 via B.~Shortest-path tree::::Edges AB and AC total 5, preserving both source distances.', 'MST weight 4; this shortest-path tree weight 5.','The tree minimizing sum of source distances is not required to minimize sum of selected edge weights.')
T('4-bfs-dfs','bfs','BFS','BFS distance layers and enqueue-time discovery',r'''
Breadth-first search begins at a source with distance zero and puts it in a FIFO queue. On removing u, inspect its neighbors; for each undiscovered v, mark it immediately, assign parent u and distance d[u]+1, then enqueue it. Marking when enqueued prevents several parents from scheduling the same vertex.
The queue processes layers in nondecreasing distance. Before a distance-k vertex is removed, every shorter-layer opportunity has already been processed, proving that first discovery gives a minimum-edge route. This proof uses unit edge costs; arbitrary weighted shortest paths require another algorithm. Directed BFS follows only outgoing edges.
With adjacency lists, initialization and complete traversal cost Θ(V+E); matrices commonly require Θ(V²). Unreachable vertices keep infinity/no parent. For a disconnected graph, restart at unvisited vertices when finding all components, but distance-from-one-source semantics remain separate. Neighbor order can change parents among equal-length routes without changing distances. GATE traces should specify tie order before asking for one exact queue sequence.
''','Edges A–B,A–C,B–D,C–D. BFS from A, considering B before C.',r'Layer zero::::Remove A and enqueue B,C at distance one.~Layer one::::Remove B and discover D at distance two; then C finds D already marked.~Result::d(D)=2,\quad parent(D)=B::The route A,B,D is one shortest path.', 'D is distance 2 and is enqueued once.','A,C,D is equally short; changing neighbor order can change the chosen parent without changing distance.')
T('4-bfs-dfs','dfs','DFS;Topological order','DFS nesting, cycles and topological order',r'''
Depth-first search follows an unvisited neighbor recursively before returning to siblings. Discovery and finish times record when a vertex enters and leaves the active stack. Descendant intervals nest inside ancestor intervals. For a directed graph, an edge to an active ancestor is a back edge and certifies a directed cycle; an edge to any merely visited vertex does not automatically do so.
For undirected DFS, ignore the edge back to the parent when testing a cycle, since the reverse incidence is not a new cycle. After each root is finished, start another at an unvisited vertex to cover the whole graph. Neighbor order affects exact timestamps and tree edges.
A topological order places every directed prerequisite before its dependent. It exists exactly for DAGs. In a DAG, reverse DFS finish order is topological; a queue-based alternative repeatedly removes zero-indegree vertices. If not all vertices can be removed, a cycle remains. A topological order need not be unique and is not the same as numerical sorting or BFS distance order.
''','DAG edges A→C,B→C,C→D. Find a topological order.',r'Initial indegrees::::A and B have zero, C has two and D has one.~Remove available vertices::::Take A then B, reducing C’s indegree to zero.~Finish::::Take C then D, giving A,B,C,D.', 'A,B,C,D is valid; B,A,C,D is also valid.','Every directed edge points from an earlier to a later position. Swapping unrelated A and B preserves this requirement.')
T('4-minimum-spanning','cuts','Minimum spanning trees;Cut property','Spanning trees, cut safety and tie subtleties',r'''
A spanning tree uses every vertex, remains connected and contains no cycle, so it has V−1 edges. An MST minimizes their total weight in a connected undirected graph. Negative weights are allowed. A cut partitions vertices into two nonempty parts; every connecting tree must cross it somewhere.
A lightest crossing edge is safe for some MST: add it to an MST omitting it, then remove a no-lighter crossing edge from the resulting cycle. If uniquely lightest, it belongs to every MST. Dually, a uniquely heaviest cycle edge cannot belong to any MST; equality weakens that conclusion. Distinct weights guarantee uniqueness but are not necessary for it.
To verify an answer, count edges, check connectedness and cycle absence, and justify selected weights by safe choices or exchanges. Small total weight without connectivity is not a spanning tree. A disconnected graph requires a forest formulation. GATE questions about “some” versus “every” MST often hinge on tied weights, so use the exact quantifier.
''','Triangle edges AB=1, BC=1, AC=1. Is every edge mandatory?',r'Construct trees::::Any two edges connect the three vertices without a cycle.~Weights::w(T)=1+1=2::All three choices are minimum.~Mandatory test::::Each particular edge is omitted by one minimum tree.', 'Every edge appears in some MST; no edge appears in every MST.','There are ties, so being a lightest cut edge does not alone make that edge compulsory.')
T('4-minimum-spanning','kruskal','Kruskal;Disjoint sets','Kruskal’s algorithm and union–find',r'''
Kruskal considers edges in nondecreasing weight, adding an edge when its endpoints currently belong to different selected-edge components. This preserves acyclicity and merges components; stop after V−1 accepted edges for a connected graph. Sorting costs O(E log E), while a disjoint-set structure makes cycle checks efficient.
Union–find maintains component representatives. Find reports a representative and union merges two distinct sets. Path compression shortens traversed parent chains, and union by rank/size controls tree growth. These parent pointers belong to the data structure; they are not necessarily graph edges or the MST’s traversal parents.
A rejected edge still contributes to processing work but not to tree weight. With equal weights, several consideration orders may yield different MSTs of equal optimum weight. Initially each vertex, including an isolated one, has its own component. If edges run out with multiple components, no full spanning tree exists. Trace representatives before union rather than judging cycles only from visual adjacency.
''','Edges AB=1,BC=2,AC=3,CD=4.',r'Accept first edges::::AB merges A,B; BC merges that component with C.~Reject cycle::::AC now connects two vertices already in the same component.~Attach D::::CD joins the remaining vertex; total weight is 1+2+4=7.', 'MST edges AB,BC,CD with weight 7.','Four vertices require three accepted edges; rejecting AC keeps the chosen set acyclic.')
T('4-minimum-spanning','prim','Prim','Prim grows one tree across its current cut',r'''
Prim starts with one vertex and repeatedly takes a cheapest edge from the current tree to an outside vertex. The selected edge crosses the tree/outside cut, so the cut property proves safety. An edge entirely inside the tree is no longer a candidate even if it is globally cheap.
A common implementation stores for each outside vertex the cheapest edge connecting it to the tree, using a min-priority queue. These keys are attachment edge costs, not distances from the start; confusing them turns the update into Dijkstra’s relaxation. Matrix implementations take O(V²), and appropriate list/heap implementations commonly O(E log V).
Different starting vertices or tie choices can yield different MSTs when weights repeat. A disconnected input cannot be covered by one growth process; restart for a forest if that is the requested output. Negative edges are fine because cut safety does not require nonnegative values. During a trace, update keys from newly added vertices and compare only crossing edges, not every edge in the input.
''','AB=4, AC=2, BC=1. Run Prim from A.',r'First cut::::From {A}, choose AC=2 over AB=4.~Update B::::From newly added C, edge CB=1 improves B’s attachment key from 4 to 1.~Finish::w(T)=2+1=3::Add BC, covering all vertices.', 'Tree edges AC and CB, weight 3.','B’s key is 1, while its path distance from A in this tree is 3; Prim keys are not source distances.')
T('4-shortest-paths','dijkstra','Shortest paths;Dijkstra','Dijkstra’s nonnegative-edge proof and relaxation',r'''
Shortest paths minimize the sum of edge weights along a route. Initialize source distance to zero and others to infinity. Relaxing u→v tries d[u]+w(u,v) and updates v and its predecessor when smaller. Dijkstra repeatedly finalizes the unprocessed vertex with smallest tentative distance, then relaxes its outgoing edges.
Nonnegative edges justify finalization: any route through a still-unprocessed vertex cannot produce a cheaper detour into the finalized one. A negative edge invalidates this proof even without a negative cycle. Zero edges are permitted. Infinity must not be treated as an ordinary finite integer that can overflow when added to a weight.
Using a heap changes time complexity and can create stale queue entries, which must be skipped or managed by decrease-key. Exact processing order can depend on tied distances, but final distances remain correct under the assumptions. Unreachable vertices have no finite route. Separate an actual predecessor path from a numeric bound and verify the reported route’s weight matches its final distance.
''','Directed edges S→A=4,S→B=1,B→A=2.',r'Initialize and process S::d(A)=4,\quad d(B)=1::Both are tentative.~Finalize B::d(A)\leftarrow\min(4,1+2)=3::The cheaper route goes through B.~Finalize A::d(A)=3::No later nonnegative detour can improve it.', 'Shortest S-to-A path is S,B,A with cost 3.','Adding the route’s edge weights gives 1+2=3, agreeing with the stored distance.')
T('4-shortest-paths','bellman','Bellman Ford','Bellman–Ford and reachable negative cycles',r'''
Bellman–Ford repeatedly relaxes every edge rather than relying on Dijkstra’s finalization. After k full passes, it correctly accounts for shortest routes requiring at most k edges under the standard argument. Without a reachable negative cycle, a shortest simple path uses at most V−1 edges, so that many passes suffice; early termination is possible when no relaxation changes anything.
An additional improving pass signals a negative cycle reachable from the source. A destination has unbounded-below route cost only if such a cycle can also reach it. An unreachable negative cycle does not invalidate every distance from this source. This reachability qualification is part of the result, not an optional detail.
Negative edges alone do not make a shortest path nonexistent. A DAG can contain negative edges and still be solved in topological order because cycles are impossible. Bellman–Ford’s standard time is O(VE). In GATE traces, edge-processing order can allow several improvements in one pass, but the bound and cycle criterion do not rely on one special order.
''','Edges S→A=2,A→B=−4,B→A=1.',r'Cycle weight::w(A\to B\to A)=-4+1=-3::Each loop lowers route cost.~Reachability::::S reaches A, so the cycle is reachable from the source.~Consequence::::Repeating the cycle yields arbitrarily small costs to A and B; a later relaxation keeps improving.', 'No finite shortest distance to A or B.','The problem is the negative cycle, not simply the presence of one negative edge.')
T('4-shortest-paths','floyd','Floyd Warshall','Floyd–Warshall’s intermediate-vertex dynamic program',r'''
Floyd–Warshall computes all-pairs distances. Let D⁽ᵏ⁾ᵢⱼ be the shortest route from i to j whose intermediate vertices come only from the first k vertices. A best route either avoids k or passes through it, yielding min(D⁽ᵏ⁻¹⁾ᵢⱼ,D⁽ᵏ⁻¹⁾ᵢₖ+D⁽ᵏ⁻¹⁾ₖⱼ). Initialize direct edge weights, zero diagonal and infinity for absent edges, while respecting supplied negative loops.
The k loop must be outermost in the ordinary implementation because it establishes the allowed-intermediate invariant. Merely applying the minimum formula in arbitrary loop order does not automatically produce the same result. Standard complexity is O(V³) time and O(V²) stored distances.
Negative edges are allowed; a negative diagonal after closure signals a negative cycle. Pairs that can route through it need special interpretation rather than an ordinary finite shortest-path answer. Guard infinity arithmetic so absent routes cannot create fake finite costs. Predecessor or next-hop data can reconstruct routes, but requires consistent updates when an improvement is selected.
''','A→B=3,B→C=2,A→C=9. Update when B becomes an allowed intermediate.',r'Previous direct distance::D_{AC}=9::The direct edge is the current bound.~Route through B::D_{AB}+D_{BC}=3+2=5::Both constituent routes are available.~Update::D_{AC}\leftarrow\min(9,5)=5::Allowing B improves the pair distance.', 'A-to-C distance becomes 5.','The reconstructed route A,B,C uses only the newly allowed intermediate B.')
